  m_Text: Electromagnetic induction is the process of producing an electric current
    by changing the magnetic field through a conductor. When a magnet moves toward
    or away from a coil of wire, the magnetic flux through the coil changes, inducing
    a voltage. A faster change in the magnetic field produces a greater induced current.
